WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.375
BUKOLA AYODELE:
Compliance audits

00:00:01.375 --> 00:00:05.250
can be arduous, painstaking,
time consuming tasks.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:08.310
Let's see how Policy
Controller simplified Oma's job

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:11.580
enough for her to leave work
before dinner time on an audit

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:12.630
day.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:13.650
Its audit day.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.740
And as people responsible
for performing audits know,

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:18.750
this is a time
every month when Oma

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:20.520
feels overwhelmed,
because she needs

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.760
to review whether the entire
company's infrastructure is

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.260
compliant.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.330
This typically
involves many hours

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:30.600
of reviewing configurations,
meetings with team leaders,

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.880
and analyzing running
infrastructure to make

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.430
sure everything is compliant.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:38.190
A lot of the time spent
tracking down potential problems

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:41.250
would disappear if there is a
way to automatically enforce

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.010
correct policy instead
of having to track down

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.152
possible compliance issues.

00:00:46.152 --> 00:00:48.360
It would be better to have
a system that stopped them

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.290
from occurring in
the first place.

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:52.170
Oma heard a lot
about the new tools

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.510
that Charlie's team
introduced and is

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:58.320
wondering if those tools
can possibly help them.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.780
After hearing their
issues, Charlie

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.230
explains Policy Controller
and how it can not only

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.630
ensure compliance,
but also enable

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:09.600
audit logging of violations
that were uncovered

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.790
when policies were introduced.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:14.070
Oma is intrigued
to say the least.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:17.370
Policy Controller is a
Kubernetes dynamic admission

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:21.570
controller that checks, audits,
and enforces your cluster

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:25.710
compliance with policies related
to security, regulations,

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.350
or arbitrary business rules.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:31.260
Policy Controller enforces
cluster compliance

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.140
with policies
called constraints.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.060
Constraints are a
powerful feature

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:39.910
that allow for fine grain
control over a cluster.

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:43.230
For example, you can enforce
many of the same requirements

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:46.740
as pod security policies,
but with the added ability

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:50.190
to audit your configurations
before enforcing it.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:51.810
You can limit the
type of volumes

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:54.360
that can be mounted
to pods, or even

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.420
control the repository a
given container image can

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:00.660
be pulled from, and more.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.820
Along with constraints,
Policy Controller

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:05.670
also introduces
constraint templates.

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.789
These allow you to define how a
constraint works with delegate

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:11.039
defining the specifics
of the constraints

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:15.450
to an individual or group
with subject matter expertise.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:17.260
In addition to
separating concerns,

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.130
this also separates the
logic of the constraints

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:21.880
from its definition.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:25.020
So for example, if you have
a family of applications

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.150
that will have to use a
limited range of ports,

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:31.920
you can define a template
like this to limit the range

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.160
but define that range with a
constraint at implementation

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.000
time.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.010
Oma's team decides
to start requiring

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:42.690
labels on namespace that specify
who the namespace owner is.

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:47.040
This is a good use of the K8s'
required labels constraints.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:50.070
In order to start using Policy
Controller to add a required

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:53.520
label, Oma first creates
a corresponding constraint

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.710
with the dry run mode set.

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:58.590
This allows Oma to
monitor all the violations

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.240
without causing downtime.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.760
Once this constraint is
applied to the cluster,

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.560
Oma can view the
audit by looking

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.050
at the constraint object logs.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:11.610
She now has a list of teams
and services to talk to.

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:15.520
After they fix the issues, she
removes the enforcement action,

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:18.270
which means the default
enforcement actions [INAUDIBLE]

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:21.570
takes effect, even stop,
further policy violations

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:23.490
from occurring.

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:25.260
In the past, she used
to have to follow up

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:27.900
with teams individually
to retroactively track

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:31.440
down and fix policy violations.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.600
Now, with Policy
Controller, Oma has

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:36.900
a list of violations and a way
to block them from occurring.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:40.020
Policy Controller using a
familiar GitOps approach

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:42.240
to enable policy
rules for existing

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:46.590
conflicts and logs violations
that do occur once enabled.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:49.500
And best of all, because
of Policy Controller,

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.960
Oma got to be home
before dinner on an audit

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.550
date for the first time ever.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:56.340
So that's it for
Policy Controller.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:58.350
Make sure to check out
the description for links

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:00.090
to more resources on it.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:01.800
And like and subscribe
to the channel

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.110
for more videos
about Google Cloud.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:05.130
Thanks for watching.

